1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:03,220
In this video, I'll
give a short overview

2
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:04,915
of Linux networking.

3
00:00:04,915 --> 00:00:08,920
I want to describe some tips for

4
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:11,859
working with IP-based networking

5
00:00:11,859 --> 00:00:14,540
on Linux and on Buildroot.

6
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:19,780
If you're searching
for Linux networking,

7
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:21,340
you'll probably see
lots of references to

8
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:24,570
ifconfig and possibly to IP.

9
00:00:24,570 --> 00:00:26,890
Ifconfig was the traditional way

10
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:29,495
to manage networking on Linux.

11
00:00:29,495 --> 00:00:31,420
As of the time I'm
recording this video,

12
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:32,980
it's still available and

13
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:34,720
probably will be
available for awhile,

14
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:37,360
but IP is the new way to manage

15
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:41,020
networking and was the more
recent way that's added,

16
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:43,520
so I'll use that
for these examples.

17
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:47,120
The most basic way you'll
interact with networking

18
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:50,135
over Linux is using
the IP addr command,

19
00:00:50,135 --> 00:00:52,550
and that'll just list
the IP address details

20
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:55,950
for each of your
network adapters.

21
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:00,050
One of the things you'll
see if you type IP addr,

22
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:03,560
you'll see lo listed and
lo is the loopback port.

23
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,690
This is useful if you're going

24
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:09,260
to send network commands
to your own device.

25
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:10,880
For instance, when
we're using socket

26
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:13,025
test on our native
implementation,

27
00:01:13,025 --> 00:01:15,790
we're really using the
loopback port for this.

28
00:01:15,790 --> 00:01:17,480
There's not really anything

29
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,340
typically that they need
to configure on this.

30
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:24,170
This 127.0.0.1 is a
common loopback address

31
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:25,865
that you can use if
you want to send

32
00:01:25,865 --> 00:01:28,705
a socket command to
yourself for instance.

33
00:01:28,705 --> 00:01:31,665
The same thing as
saying local host.

34
00:01:31,665 --> 00:01:35,505
Eth0 is the local wired Ethernet

35
00:01:35,505 --> 00:01:39,200
and depending on what
system you're using,

36
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:40,730
and if you're using System D,

37
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:43,435
you might see enp
instead of eth0.

38
00:01:43,435 --> 00:01:47,910
It's just a different way to
denote the Ethernet port.

39
00:01:48,310 --> 00:01:54,070
Sit0 is the tunneling
for IPv6 over IPv4.

40
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:55,910
There won't be any
specific thing we'll

41
00:01:55,910 --> 00:01:57,980
do with this in this course.

42
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:00,140
Then WLAN is another
one that you'd

43
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:02,860
have if you are using
a wireless lan port,

44
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:06,310
if you're using Wi-Fi
in your project.

45
00:02:06,310 --> 00:02:08,830
You can see that with
each one of these,

46
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:12,240
there's a setting associated
with the IP address,

47
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:14,200
there is a MAC address that

48
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,640
defines the specific hardware,

49
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:21,700
and there's some level of
status here about the link.

50
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:25,000
Using the eth as the case,

51
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,330
this up here means
that the link is up,

52
00:02:27,330 --> 00:02:28,915
but this may or may not

53
00:02:28,915 --> 00:02:30,700
mean that you're connected
to the Internet.

54
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:33,790
This in its self is
not enough to know

55
00:02:33,790 --> 00:02:35,035
whether or not you have

56
00:02:35,035 --> 00:02:37,490
a routable Internet
address for instance.

57
00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:41,340
The link in ether section
lists the MAC address.

58
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:42,880
That's the hardware
address that's

59
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:47,110
globally unique reference
to this particular device.

60
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:48,530
In other words, there's

61
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:50,420
no other device in the
world that would have

62
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:51,965
this same MAC address

63
00:02:51,965 --> 00:02:53,690
based on the fact
that it's assigned,

64
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:56,930
given a hardware identification

65
00:02:56,930 --> 00:02:58,915
that's unique to the hardware.

66
00:02:58,915 --> 00:03:02,300
Then the inet section here gives

67
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:05,815
the IPv4 address and mask.

68
00:03:05,815 --> 00:03:09,510
We mentioned earlier
when we talked about

69
00:03:09,510 --> 00:03:13,155
socket communication
IPv4 versus IPv6,

70
00:03:13,155 --> 00:03:16,920
and this section would
give you the IPv4 address.

71
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:20,490
This inet 6 gives you
the IPv6 address.

72
00:03:20,490 --> 00:03:22,580
What you'll probably see with

73
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,020
your default hardware
build is that you

74
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:25,775
end up getting an
address like this,

75
00:03:25,775 --> 00:03:28,340
and you might wonder well,
how did that happen?

76
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:30,820
How did I get an
address assigned?

77
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:33,810
It turns out the
default setting for

78
00:03:33,810 --> 00:03:37,070
Yocto Builder is going to
use what's called DHCP,

79
00:03:37,070 --> 00:03:39,250
Dynamic Host
Configuration Protocol,

80
00:03:39,250 --> 00:03:40,660
that will automatically get you

81
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:42,610
an address from your router,

82
00:03:42,610 --> 00:03:44,060
assuming your
router supports it,

83
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:46,155
and most routers do today.

84
00:03:46,155 --> 00:03:48,310
This is all set up with

85
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:51,490
a default configuration in
etc network interfaces,

86
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:54,190
and this example
showing builder,

87
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:56,950
it shows that this file is
automatically generated,

88
00:03:56,950 --> 00:03:58,690
and if you go into your
system configuration,

89
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:01,270
you can see where it's
configured here to use

90
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:04,195
DHCP for eth0 by default.

91
00:04:04,195 --> 00:04:07,090
This is on the Raspberry
Pi for instance.

92
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:09,505
It'll be a similar setup on

93
00:04:09,505 --> 00:04:12,160
different hardware
devices, and usually,

94
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:14,720
you'll find that the
primary network connection

95
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:17,770
is going to be set up for
you in using Ethernet.

96
00:04:17,770 --> 00:04:20,465
For more complex configurations,

97
00:04:20,465 --> 00:04:24,170
you can edit this etc
network interfaces file for

98
00:04:24,170 --> 00:04:28,085
instance by using a root
file system overlay,

99
00:04:28,085 --> 00:04:29,150
like what we did for one of the

100
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:30,545
assignments in Buildroot,

101
00:04:30,545 --> 00:04:32,450
or if you're using Yocto,

102
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:34,685
you can use ROOTFS
post-process command

103
00:04:34,685 --> 00:04:36,845
to change the content
of this file.

104
00:04:36,845 --> 00:04:39,860
If you don't need a
custom IP address,

105
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,555
you can just use DHCP

106
00:04:41,555 --> 00:04:45,389
and use a router
that supports DHCP.